Also, with limited unshown T6 (2 left according to counts), Peng might be unnecessary if I can later use T6 for something else.  \n3. Considering scoring: I need at least 8 points. Currently, I have one Chow (T6-8), which gives no points by itself. If I Peng T6, I could aim for All Pungs (6 points) + maybe Tile Hog (2 points if I keep four T6? But after Peng, I'd have three, not four) or other combinations. But with only 9 tiles left in hand after Peng+discard, it's challenging.  \n4. Passing keeps my hand concealed and allows drawing new tiles to improve toward a better shape, like seeking chows or pairs.  \n\nGiven the situation, I choose to Pass because my hand is not yet ready for a high-scoring win, and Peng might commit me to a path that's hard to complete with the available tiles.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
